arty on other  issues but on judicial nominations  that's something we've seen them be  lockstep with the other democrats on and  i suspect something this big and this  historic that they will continue to  support biden's judicial nominees what  about republicans then i know you  mentioned that you only need 50 votes  with the vice president breaking the tie  but is there any way for republicans in  the senate to derail this or might we  even see some  republican senators perhaps cross party  lines and vote with the democrats  well crossing party lines of voting with  democrats is something that we just  don't see anymore um i do think that  with president biden suggesting that  he'll not  eliminate the court's first black woman  that might uh change the calculus a  little bit for some republicans um  though i do think it'll be pretty close  to that 50 vote threshold um and but you  know again that's something we have to  go wait and see i don't think that  there's anything procedurally  republicans can do at this point  but uh former senate  leader mitch mcconnell has already said  that if this nomination is still open  after the midterm elections and if  republicans take over the senate that  they will  block president biden's nominee so i'm  sure that the white house is working a  pretty quick speed to make sure that  doesn't happen  i'm curious too you talked about the  filibuster another potential shift  that's come up for the biden  administration is term limits for  supreme court justices whatever happened  to that can you update us on that whole  dynamic  sure so uh the president did put in  place the supreme court commission and  they came out with their findings but  the findings weren't ever intended to be  a recommendation instead they were more  like a book report the pros and cons of  term limits and since that's come out we  haven't heard anything from the white  house we don't even have a timeline on  um you know what the supreme court or  what the white house might do with those  uh recommendations and findings so so  far we're on a holding pattern on those  i will say that you know groups who  interest groups who watch the supreme  court still have expanding the court top  of mind but i wonder if having this  nominee may kind of bring the  temperature down on that issue  interesting especially since there were  a lot of groups that were um  i wouldn't say picketing but really  pushing the idea that briar should step  down last summer and they obviously  didn't get anywhere with that at the  time  give us a little bit more of a sense of  who justice stephen breyer is because  he's 83 he's been uh the justice uh the  longest-serving justice at the moment  and i feel like we don't know a whole  lot about him  well that's right and one of the reasons  that we don't know a lot about him is  that justice breyer actually spent 11  years as the court's most junior justice  and so he didn't really write a lot of  the bigger um cases that we think of  when we think of a justice who have  served on the supreme court this long  instead his legacy has really been kind  of behind the scenes creating consensus  so i think that's going to be a big part  of his legacy but another thing is that  he's a very pragmatic justice where a  lot of the other justices want to put  brightline rules you know does a certain  issue fall on one side of the line or  another justice breyer is a little more  wishy-washy and wants to weigh lots of  factors so that's kind of an element i  think that uh we'll be missing without  justice breyer on the court um but you  know and for me personally one thing  that i will miss as a supreme court  reporter who's in the courtroom a lot is  justice breyer is just known for his  crazy off-the-wall hypotheticals yeah  that really make um listening to oral  arguments interesting so that's  something personally i'm going to be  interested in and i think will also be  part of his legacy as well yeah kimberly  i'm a huge hypotheticals nerd this is  like the way that my friends and i hang  out with each other is just insane  hypothetical questions walk me through  your favorites from briar what are your  all-star hypothetical moments that stick  out to you  well we actually have a video on this  that we did last year when clamor around  his retirement came and it reminded me  of some of the funniest ones he talks  about um these giant tomatoes that grow  into people and then go around  destroying cities and to be honest i  can't even tell you what legal principle  that was trying to get at i think  marijuana laws or something right i  think that's right that sounds about  right um we've also seen in products  liabilities cases him talk about you  know ice cream stands that poison their  customers um just really anything and  everything you can talk about he has a  really unique way of kind of honing in  on the particular legal issue that's  bothering him what an interesting  thinker who would bring up deadly tomato  children or you know  ice cream stands at that are deadly so  let's continue with this hypothetical  idea um of course everyone's going to be  wondering who's on the white house this  short list and you know i think back to  episodes of the west wing where they  pull out a list that they've already  drawn up and are going through all the  names and and bring them into the white  house to interview them and and do all  the due diligence what can you tell us  about who is at the top of the list you  mentioned  president biden had promised to nominate  a black woman  that's right and i think this is one  reason why the process could move along  pretty quickly i think common thinking  is that there are two women  at the top of the white house's list  although some others who may be um  kind of vying for an underdog seat and  that's leandra kruger who currently sits  as a justice of the california supreme  court and uh federal judge contanji  brown jackson who was 